@flexiarg institutions/collective-choice-arrangements
@title Collective-Choice Arrangements
@keywords collective choice, rule change, participation, Ostrom 3, common-pool resources
@audience institution designers, commons researchers
@tone normative
@style pattern
@grade principle
@provenance Ostrom design principle 3, wording as in Cox, Arnold and Tomas (2010), "A review of design principles for community-based natural resource management", Ecology and Society 15(4):38, quoted in Corneli 2016 (ICCC 2016) Table 1. Original source Ostrom 1990, Governing the Commons. Drafted by claude-16 with Joe, 2026-10-29.
@status draft 2026-10-29; to vet against Ostrom 1990 and Cox et al. 2010 before relying on non-quoted text
@references [institutions/ARGUMENT creativity-commons/affected-participants-change-the-rules]

! conclusion:
  "Most individuals affected by the operational rules can participate in modifying the operational rules."

  + context:
    Operational rules govern day-to-day use; collective-choice rules govern how operational rules are changed.

  + IF:
    Operational rules are set for a resource and the users meet cases the rules handle badly.

  + HOWEVER:
    Participation is lossy in practice: changes tend to be made by those most affected, and therefore most knowledgeable (Ostrom et al. 2012, p. 79).

  + THEN:
    Give most of those affected by the operational rules a way to take part in changing them.

  + BECAUSE:
    Those who use the resource hold local knowledge about it, and rules they helped make fit better and are more readily followed.

  + NEXT-STEPS:
    next[Vet the HOWEVER and BECAUSE against Ostrom 1990 and Cox et al. 2010; only the conclusion is quoted.]
    next[Creativity-commons transposition: creativity-commons/affected-participants-change-the-rules.]
